# <%= app_name %>

Automated playtesting for tabletop games with [PlAI Testr](https://github.com/armahillo/plai_testr): local LLM players (via [Ollama](https://ollama.com)) and scripted bots play your game, and every session ends in a markdown report.

## Quick start

```sh
bundle install

# Bots only, no model needed: 100 games in a few seconds, then a report
bundle exec plai_testr hearts botrun --games 100

# One game with a local model (needs Ollama running; missing models are pulled)
bundle exec plai_testr gin_rummy play
```

Reports go in `games/<game>/reports/`; resumable sessions in `games/<game>/sessions/`.

## Commands

```
bundle exec plai_testr <game> play      [--seed N] [--model M] [--mode llm|hybrid|bot]  One game (random seed 1-100 unless given)
bundle exec plai_testr <game> botrun    [--games N] [--personas all]                   Bots only, many games
bundle exec plai_testr <game> resume    <session_id>                                   Continue an interrupted session
bundle exec plai_testr <game> debrief   <session_id> [--model M] [--think]             Ask a finished game's players the debrief again
bundle exec plai_testr <game> preflight                                                Check Ollama, pull and warm up models, lint
bundle exec plai_testr <game> compare   [--since ID]                                   Compare models across sessions
bundle exec plai_testr <game> list                                                     List sessions
bundle exec plai_testr generate game <name>                                            Add a new game
bundle exec plai_testr games                                                           List the games
```

Every command takes `--help` to list its options, e.g. `bundle exec plai_testr hearts botrun --help`. `play --debrief-model M --debrief-think` gives the end-of-game debrief its own model and thinking.

`--quiet` shows only a progress line and the result; `--verbose` shows more. Ctrl+C once saves and stops after the current move (a botrun finishes its current game and reports); Ctrl+C twice quits immediately.

Settings for the whole app (decision limit, model, Ollama host, context size) are in `config/plai_testr.rb`.

## Layout

```
Gemfile
AGENTS.md                 Notes for AI assistants specific to this app (CLAUDE.md imports it and the gem's guide)
.plai_testr/gem           Link to the installed plai_testr gem: its guide, README, docs and examples (git-ignored)
config/plai_testr.rb      App-wide settings
spec/spec_helper.rb       Loads every game under games/
games/
  hearts/                 Example: 4-player trick-taking
  gin_rummy/              Example: 2-player rummy
```

Each game lives entirely in its own directory:

```
games/<name>/
  lib/<name>.rb           Requires the game and registers it with PlaiTestr
  lib/<name>/bot.rb       Bot rules: how a bot picks moves
  lib/<name>/             Runner (rules engine), state classes
  data/rules.json         Machine-readable rules: game name, version, player count, card deck
  data/rules.md           Rules text the model players read
  data/personas.yml       Optional: strategy and play styles for the model players
  data/bots.yml           Optional: parameters for the bot rules
  NOTES.md                Optional: open rules questions and unconfirmed engine choices
  spec/                   The game's specs
  sessions/ reports/      Output (gitignored)
```

## Working with an AI assistant

The PlAI Testr guide (`.plai_testr/gem/docs/AGENTS.md`, kept current with the installed gem) tells an AI coding assistant how this project works: where the docs are, how to build a harness, and what to ask you before doing. `AGENTS.md` adds notes specific to this app. Claude Code reads both through `CLAUDE.md`; run `claude` in this directory and start talking.

Some ways to start:

- *Look around this project and tell me what's here and how to run it.*
- *I want to playtest my game. The rules are in ~/designs/my_game/rules.md. Read them, list every rules question you have, then set up a harness for it.*
- *Write bot rules for my_game so it can play itself, run a 1000-game botrun, and summarize the report.*
- *Read the newest report in games/my_game/reports and tell me what it says about balance. Separate what might be the bot's habits from what might be the game.*
- *The model players in my_game keep making the same bad choice. Show me the prompt they see and suggest how to make the menu clearer, without telling them what to pick.*
- *I've ruled that a tie goes to the defender. Update the engine, add a spec, and record the ruling in data/rules.md.*
- *Add personas to my_game: one player who races, one who blocks.*

## Adding a game

```sh
bundle exec plai_testr generate game my_game
```

This creates the skeleton above. Fill in `data/rules.json` and `data/rules.md`, then implement the runner hooks (`setup`, `legal_moves`, `apply_action_effects`) and, optionally, bot rules in `bot_choice`. The examples show complete games; the [PlAI Testr README](https://github.com/armahillo/plai_testr#writing-a-game) lists every hook.

`bundle exec rspec games/my_game` runs its specs. The generated botrun spec stays pending until the game can play itself to the end.

## Removing a game

Delete its directory: `rm -rf games/hearts`. Nothing else refers to it.

Keep all of a game's files under `games/<name>/` so that stays true. If a game ever needs files somewhere else (for example `spec/<name>/`), add a rake task that removes them along with the game directory.
